---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '~/components/Figure.astro';
import Assumptions from '~/components/Assumptions.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import Math from '~/components/Math.astro';
import Badge from '~/components/Badge.astro';
import Cite from '~/components/Cite.astro';
import SevenNetDomains from '~/islands/SevenNetDomains';
import SevenNetParallel from '~/islands/SevenNetParallel';
import SevenNetFidelity from '~/islands/SevenNetFidelity';
import quartz from '~/data/structures/sio2-alpha-quartz.json';
import snapshot from '~/data/benchmarks/matbench-discovery.json';
import type { Structure } from '~/lib/structure/types';
import { PAPER_BENCH, SEVENNET0, decompositionCost, domainEdge, shellCounts } from '~/lib/models/sevennet';
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import type { Checkpoint } from '~/content/schemas';

const q = quartz as unknown as Structure;

// Build-time table: one rank of the paper's weak-scaling runs, and SevenNet-0's settings (same module as Fig. 1).
const rows = [
  ...[2, 3, 4, 5].map((T) => ({ label: `benchmark models, ${T} layers`, rc: PAPER_BENCH.rc, T })),
  { label: 'SevenNet-0 settings, 5 layers', rc: SEVENNET0.rc, T: SEVENNET0.layers },
].map(({ label, rc, T }) => {
  const c = shellCounts(q, domainEdge(q, PAPER_BENCH.atomsPerGpu), rc, T);
  return { label, rc, T, k: decompositionCost(c, T, SEVENNET0.featureFloats) };
});

const ck0 = (await getEntry('checkpoints', 'sevennet-0-11jul2024'))!.data as Checkpoint;
const ckMf = (await getEntry('checkpoints', 'sevennet-mf-ompa'))!.data as Checkpoint;
const board = snapshot.models.filter((m) => m.family_dir === 'sevennet');

const domainAssumptions = [
  { term: 'Crystal', value: 'α-quartz built with ASE from the room-pressure refinement of Levien, Prewitt & Weidner (1980): a = 4.916 Å, c = 5.4054 Å, 9 atoms per cell, 2.65 g/cm³. The paper benchmarks on α-quartz too.' },
  { term: 'Domain', value: 'One rank’s subdomain is a cube holding the chosen number of atoms on average, placed in the infinite crystal; every rank of a large homogeneous run sees the same picture. The 3D view is a 5.4 Å slice through its middle.' },
  { term: 'Ghosts', value: 'As LAMMPS builds them from a communication cutoff: every atom inside the box extended by r_c (SevenNet) or by T·r_c (no exchange between layers) on each side.' },
  { term: 'Work', value: 'Node updates per force call: SevenNet updates its own atoms on each of T layers. Without exchange, layer t must be evaluated for every atom within (T − t)·r_c of the box. Counts of node updates, not timings; per-edge cost, GPU utilisation and latency are not modelled.' },
  { term: 'Traffic', value: 'Floats arriving per rank per force call: 4 per ghost (position, species), 480 per ghost for each of the T − 1 feature exchanges and the T − 1 gradient exchanges (SevenNet-0’s 128x0e + 64x1e + 32x2e), 3 per ghost for partial forces. Floats are counted, not bytes; message latency is not counted.' },
];
const toyAssumptions = [
  { term: 'System', value: '300 atoms of two species on a jittered triangular lattice (spacing 1, toy units) in a free rectangle; strips along x are the subdomains.' },
  { term: 'Model', value: 'An invariant message-passing network: 4 channels, edge filters from 4 Bessel functions times NequIP’s p = 6 polynomial cutoff at r_c = 1.8, m_v = Σ_w f_t(r_vw) ⊙ W_t h_w, h_v ← h_v + tanh(U_t m_v), E_v = q·h_v + shift(Z_v). Random untrained weights; energies in arbitrary units.' },
  { term: 'Forces', value: 'Hand-written reverse mode, checked against finite differences. The parallel runs share nothing but what the chosen exchanges send.' },
  { term: 'Agreement', value: '“Identical” means energy within 10⁻⁹ relative and every force within 10⁻⁹ of the largest force; measured differences are about 10⁻¹² (sevennet.test.ts).' },
];
const fidAssumptions = [
  { term: 'Model', value: 'The toy network of Fig. 2 with three layers. Fidelity 0 is the plain model; fidelity 1 adds a random bias vector in the embedding and in each update layer (“every linear layer”) and a per-species energy shift.' },
  { term: 'Not shown', value: 'Training. In SevenNet-MF the fidelity weights start at zero and are fitted together with the shared weights on all databases at once.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="SevenNet"
  description="SevenNet: NequIP's equivariant architecture made to run across many GPUs in LAMMPS by exchanging ghost-atom features and gradients between layers, and SevenNet-MF's multi-fidelity training. Ghost shells counted in α-quartz, a parallel toy network checked against serial, checkpoints and limits."
>
  <article class="page-grid model-page">
    <header class="wide head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/models/">Models</a> · Model family</p>
      <h1 class="h-display">SevenNet</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Large molecular-dynamics codes split a simulation box into subdomains, one per processor, each holding a thin shell of
        “ghost” copies of its neighbours’ atoms. A message-passing network sees further than its cutoff, which breaks that
        arrangement. SevenNet, the Scalable EquiVariance-Enabled Neural Network, keeps <a href="/models/nequip/">NequIP</a>’s
        architecture and changes how it is evaluated: ghost node features travel forward between layers and their energy
        gradients travel back, so the shell stays one cutoff thick and multi-GPU runs reproduce the serial model
        <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Abstract; Sec. 3" />. A later extension, SevenNet-MF, trains one model on databases
        computed at different levels of theory <Cite id="kim2024multifidelity" locator="Sec. 2.1" />.
      </p>
      <dl class="glance">
        <div><dt>Symmetry</dt><dd>Exact E(3) equivariance, as NequIP (e3nn tensor products)</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Locality</dt><dd>T × r<sub>c</sub>; SevenNet-0: 5 × 5 Å. Ghost shell in parallel runs: r<sub>c</sub></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Messages</dt><dd>NequIP convolutions; one TorchScript module per layer</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Forces</dt><dd>Conservative: −∂E/∂r, assembled from edge forces</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Introduced</dt><dd>J. Chem. Theory Comput. 2024; multi-fidelity J. Am. Chem. Soc. 2024</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Code</dt><dd><a href="https://github.com/MDIL-SNU/SevenNet">SevenNet</a> (PyTorch, LAMMPS; MIT)</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="rule-note"><Badge label="schematic" /> This page is about the <strong>architecture and its parallel evaluation</strong>; trained checkpoints are listed separately below.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="mp-h">
      <h2 id="mp-h" class="h-section">Message passing and the receptive field</h2>
      <p>
        Like most interatomic potentials, SevenNet writes the energy as a sum of atomic energies and the forces as its negative
        gradient <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Eqs. (1)–(2)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="1" tex={String.raw`E = \sum_{i=1}^{N} E_i, \qquad \mathbf F_i = -\nabla_i E`} />
      <p>
        The authors stress that spatial decomposition is valid only because of this split. Atomic energies come from node features
        updated by <i>T</i> message-passing layers, each with a message function <i>M<sub>t</sub></i> and an update function
        <i>U<sub>t</sub></i> <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 2.1; Eqs. (3)–(4)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="2" tex={String.raw`m^{(t+1)}_v = \sum_{w \in \mathcal N(v)} M_t\big(h^{(t)}_v, h^{(t)}_w, e_{vw}\big), \qquad h^{(t+1)}_v = U_t\big(h^{(t)}_v, m^{(t+1)}_v\big)`} />
      <p>
        Edges join atoms closer than <i>r</i><sub>c</sub>, so <i>h</i><sup>(<i>t</i>+1)</sup> depends on atoms up to <i>t</i> cutoffs
        away and <i>E<sub>v</sub></i> on atoms up to <i>T</i>·<i>r</i><sub>c</sub> away (the <a href="/models/nequip/">NequIP</a> page
        shows this growth in a real crystal). In SevenNet the convolution block of NequIP is the message function and the
        interaction block the update <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 2.2; Fig. 2" />. SevenNet-0 uses five interaction
        blocks with 128 scalar, 64 vector and 32 rank-2 features and <i>r</i><sub>c</sub> = 5 Å, so each atomic energy depends on
        atoms 25 Å away <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 5.1" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="dd-h">
      <h2 id="dd-h" class="h-section">Why message passing breaks spatial decomposition</h2>
      <p>
        In spatial decomposition each processor evaluates energies and forces of the atoms in its own subdomain and receives
        positions of atoms near its boundary as ghosts. For a strictly local potential, ghosts within <i>r</i><sub>c</sub> are enough.
        A message-passing network would need ghosts within <i>T</i>·<i>r</i><sub>c</sub>, and neighbouring processors would then
        recompute the large subgraph they share <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 1" />. Figure 1 counts what that means
        in quartz.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="One rank’s atoms and ghosts in α-quartz" labels={['live-calculation']} number={1} id="fig-domains" framed={false}>
        <SevenNetDomains client:visible quartz={q} />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          A subdomain owned by one GPU and the ghost atoms it must hold: within r<sub>c</sub> when node features are exchanged
          between layers (SevenNet), within T·r<sub>c</sub> when they are not. With five 5 Å layers and the paper’s 4,608 atoms per
          GPU, the deep shell holds eleven ghosts for every owned atom and the rank does four times the node updates. SevenNet
          avoids both and pays in traffic instead: more data, in more exchanges.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={domainAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Communication pattern from <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 3.1; Fig. 3" />; SevenNet-0 settings from its <code>pre_train.yaml</code>. Counted in your browser by <code>src/lib/models/sevennet.ts</code> (checks in <code>sevennet.test.ts</code>).</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="bench-h">
      <h3 id="bench-h" class="h-sub">At the paper’s 4,608 atoms per GPU</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="ck">
          <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>r<sub>c</sub></th><th>Owned</th><th>Ghosts, SevenNet</th><th>Ghosts, T·r<sub>c</sub></th><th>Node updates, naive ÷ SevenNet</th><th>Floats received, SevenNet ÷ naive</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              rows.map(({ label, rc, k }) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{label}</td>
                  <td>{rc} Å</td>
                  <td>{k.owned.toLocaleString('en')}</td>
                  <td>{k.ghostsSeven.toLocaleString('en')}</td>
                  <td>{k.ghostsNaive.toLocaleString('en')}</td>
                  <td>{(k.updatesNaive / k.updatesSeven).toFixed(2)}</td>
                  <td>{(k.commSeven.total / k.commNaive.total).toFixed(0)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small">
        Computed at build time by the same module as Fig. 1 for a cubic subdomain of α-quartz. The traffic ratio assumes SevenNet-0’s
        480 floats per feature exchange for every row; the benchmark models had 4–64 channels with <i>l</i><sub>max</sub> = 3, so
        their messages were smaller or larger <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 4" />. The trade is deliberate: on GPUs
        connected by NVLink and InfiniBand, moving features is cheaper than holding and recomputing the deep shell.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="rev-h">
      <h2 id="rev-h" class="h-section">Forward features, reverse gradients</h2>
      <p>
        SevenNet’s exchange runs in both directions <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 3.1; Fig. 3a–f" />. On the forward path,
        ghost positions and species arrive first. The first layer needs nothing else, because a ghost’s initial feature is just the
        embedding of its species. After each later layer the owners send the ghosts’ updated features. The forces are harder. Each
        edge feature <i>e</i><sup>(<i>t</i>)</sup><sub><i>vw</i></sub> is built from the bond vector, so the authors define an edge force
        and assemble atomic forces from it <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Eqs. (5)–(6)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="3" tex={String.raw`\mathbf F_{vw} = \sum_{t=1}^{T} \frac{\partial E}{\partial e^{(t)}_{vw}} \frac{\partial e^{(t)}_{vw}}{\partial \mathbf r_{vw}}, \qquad \mathbf F_v = -\frac{\partial E}{\partial \mathbf r_v} = \sum_{w \in \mathcal N(v)} \big(\mathbf F_{vw} - \mathbf F_{wv}\big)`} />
      <p>
        An edge feature only enters the update of its receiving atom, so ∂<i>E</i>/∂<i>e</i> follows from ∂<i>E</i>/∂<i>h</i> of that
        atom. Those gradients come from the chain rule, layer by layer from the top <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Eqs. (7)–(8)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="4" tex={String.raw`\frac{\partial E}{\partial h^{(T)}_v} = \frac{\partial E}{\partial h^{(T+1)}_v}\frac{\partial h^{(T+1)}_v}{\partial h^{(T)}_v} + \sum_{w \in \mathcal N(v)} \frac{\partial E}{\partial h^{(T+1)}_w}\frac{\partial h^{(T+1)}_w}{\partial h^{(T)}_v}`} />
      <p>
        The second term is <i>h<sub>v</sub></i>’s influence on its neighbours’ energies. When a neighbour <i>w</i> is owned by another
        processor, that processor holds <i>v</i> as a ghost, computes the term, and must send it back: the reverse communication.
        Repeating this for every layer down to the second, and finally sending back the edge forces <b>F</b><sub><i>wv</i></sub>
        computed on ghosts, completes Eq. (3). Altogether there are <i>T</i> − 1 forward exchanges of features and <i>T</i> − 1
        reverse exchanges of gradients per force call.
      </p>
      <ol class="flow" aria-label="One SevenNet force call on one rank">
        <li><span>ghosts in</span> positions and species within r<sub>c</sub></li>
        <li class="key"><span>forward ×(T − 1)</span> layer t on owned atoms, then ghost features h in</li>
        <li><span>energy</span> sum over owned atoms, global reduction</li>
        <li class="key"><span>reverse ×(T − 1)</span> ∂E/∂h of ghosts out, summed by owners</li>
        <li><span>forces</span> ghost edge forces out, summed by owners</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        In the code each interaction block is its own TorchScript file, and LAMMPS’ communication routines run between them; the
        architecture is otherwise NequIP’s, in PyTorch with e3nn <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 3.2" />. Figure 2 runs the
        same choreography on a toy network small enough to check every number.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Parallel and serial, to the last digit" labels={['toy-model', 'live-calculation']} number={2} id="fig-parallel" framed={false}>
        <SevenNetParallel client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          A toy message-passing network run on one process and split over up to four ranks. With SevenNet’s three exchanges the
          parallel energy and forces equal the serial ones to rounding. Switching an exchange off shows what it carries: features fix
          the energy, gradients and partial forces fix the forces of atoms near the boundaries. The naive scheme needs no exchange
          between layers but repeats work.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={toyAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Exchange pattern and Eqs. (5)–(8) of <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 3.1" />. Computed in your browser by <code>src/lib/models/sevennet.ts</code>; exactness for T = 1–5 and P = 2–4 is tested in <code>sevennet.test.ts</code>.</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="scale-h">
      <h2 id="scale-h" class="h-section">What the scaling tests found</h2>
      <p>
        The authors trained 20 NequIP-type models on SiO<sub>2</sub> (4–64 channels, 2–5 layers, <i>r</i><sub>c</sub> = 4 Å) and ran
        them in LAMMPS on up to 32 A100 GPUs, in single precision <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 4" />. In weak scaling
        (4,608 atoms per GPU) efficiency at 32 GPUs was 0.67–0.83 for two layers and 0.79–0.84 for five, and it rose slightly with more
        channels and layers, because computation grew faster than communication <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 4.1; Fig. 4" />.
        Strong scaling was weaker. On 12,960 atoms the 32-channel models reached speed-ups of 13.1–15.4 on 32 GPUs, and the smallest
        model stopped gaining beyond four. The authors attribute this to GPUs left under-used as the atoms per GPU fall
        <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 4.2; Fig. 5" />. Fig. 1 shows the geometric side of the same trend: the ghost
        fraction rises steeply as domains shrink.
      </p>
      <p>
        As a demonstration, SevenNet-0 quenched a 112,000-atom Si<sub>3</sub>N<sub>4</sub> melt on 8 GPUs at about 0.1 ns per day, with a
        weak-scaling efficiency of 0.94 against a 14,000-atom run on one GPU. The amorphous structure’s radial and angular
        distributions agreed with DFT data averaged over 40 small cells <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 5.2; Fig. 6" />.
        The authors argue that the same scheme fits any network with two-body message functions (SchNet, PaiNN, MACE), while
        <a href="/models/m3gnet-chgnet/">M3GNet and CHGNet</a>, with explicit three-body terms, would also need edge features and
        their gradients exchanged <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 6" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="mf-h">
      <h2 id="mf-h" class="h-section">Multi-fidelity: one model, several levels of theory</h2>
      <p>
        Large GGA databases are cheap; meta-GGA or hybrid data are scarce. SevenNet-MF trains on both at once. A database’s
        fidelity is a rotation-invariant label, so it is one-hot encoded as l = 0 features and concatenated to the scalar input
        of every linear layer: the atom-type embedding, the self-interaction layers in the interaction blocks and the first layer of
        the output block. Each fidelity also gets its own atomic energy shift and scale. The fidelity weights start at zero, and at
        inference the user picks the fidelity to predict <Cite id="kim2024multifidelity" locator="Sec. 2.1; Fig. 1a–d" />. For a
        linear layer acting on scalar features <i>x</i>, with fidelity <i>f</i>,
      </p>
      <Math display number="5" tex={String.raw`W \begin{bmatrix} x \\ \mathbf e_f \end{bmatrix} = W_x\, x + W_{\mathrm f}\, \mathbf e_f = W_x\, x + \mathbf b_f`} />
      <p>
        so the one-hot is a fidelity-dependent bias. Because the convolutions are untouched, the parallel algorithm needs no change
        <Cite id="kim2024multifidelity" locator="Sec. 2.1" />. Where the bias sits decides what it can do.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="derivation" title="Why a bias at the end cannot change forces">
        <p id="fidelity-derivation">
          If the fidelity only adds a per-species constant to each atomic energy, <i>E<sub>f</sub></i> = <i>E</i><sub>0</sub> +
          Σ<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>s<sub>f</sub></i>(<i>Z<sub>i</sub></i>), and the extra term does not depend on any position:
        </p>
        <Math display tex={String.raw`\mathbf F^{(f)}_i = -\nabla_i E_0 - \nabla_i \sum_j s_f(Z_j) = \mathbf F^{(0)}_i`} />
        <p>
          The same holds for a model linear in fixed descriptors <i>D</i>(<b>r</b>), since <b>w</b>·<i>D</i> + <b>v</b>·<b>e</b><sub><i>f</i></sub>
          has the same gradient for every <i>f</i>; Kim et al. make this point for simplified Behler–Parrinello networks and GAP
          <Cite id="kim2024multifidelity" locator="Sec. 3" />. A bias inside a message-passing layer passes through nonlinearities
          and is multiplied by geometry-dependent filters in later layers, so ∂<i>E<sub>f</sub></i>/∂<b>r</b> depends on <i>f</i>.
          Figure 3 checks both cases, and <code>sevennet.test.ts</code> checks that the shift-only forces agree exactly.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Where the fidelity enters decides what it can change" labels={['toy-model', 'live-calculation']} number={3} id="fig-fidelity" framed={false}>
        <SevenNetFidelity client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          The same structure at two fidelities. With the fidelity only in the energy shift, energies move by a constant and every
          force is unchanged, exactly; the dimer curves are parallel. With the fidelity in every linear layer, as in SevenNet-MF, the
          two energy surfaces have different shapes and the forces differ.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={fidAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Architecture from <Cite id="kim2024multifidelity" locator="Sec. 2.1; Fig. 1c,d" /> and <code>sevenn/nn/linear.py</code> (commit 209339d). Computed in your browser by <code>src/lib/models/sevennet.ts</code>.</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="mfres-h">
      <h3 id="mfres-h" class="h-sub">What multi-fidelity training bought</h3>
      <p>
        For the Li<sub>6</sub>PS<sub>5</sub>Cl solid electrolyte, SCAN single points on a subsample of the PBE structures added only
        10–20 % to the cost of the PBE data. The multi-fidelity model trained on both gave Li-ion conductivities within 10 % of a model
        trained on a full SCAN dataset. A model trained on the SCAN subsample alone deviated and formed short Li–Li bonds above
        600 K <Cite id="kim2024multifidelity" locator="Sec. 2.2; Fig. 2; Table 1" />. For In<sub>x</sub>Ga<sub>1−x</sub>N, with r²SCAN data
        only for the binary nitrides, the multi-fidelity model predicted mixing energies of unseen alloys with R² = 0.98 against a
        high-fidelity reference. Transfer learning scored −4.55 and Δ-learning 0.31 <Cite id="kim2024multifidelity" locator="Sec. 2.2; Fig. 3" />.
        Trained on Materials Project PBE and r²SCAN relaxations, SevenNet-MF-0 corrected PBE’s errors in the Cs–Cr–Cl phase diagram
        <Cite id="kim2024multifidelity" locator="Sec. 2.3; Fig. 4c" />. The released SevenNet-MF-ompa and SevenNet-Omni models use the
        same mechanism with PBE(+U) tasks from different databases (SevenNet documentation).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="maths-h">
      <h2 id="maths-h" class="h-section">The mathematics at three depths</h2>
      <div class="depths">
        <input type="radio" name="depth" id="s-int" checked />
        <label for="s-int">Intuition</label>
        <input type="radio" name="depth" id="s-tech" />
        <label for="s-tech">Technical</label>
        <input type="radio" name="depth" id="s-res" />
        <label for="s-res">Research</label>
        <div class="depth depth-int">
          <p>
            Each GPU looks after one block of the simulation and keeps copies of the atoms just across its border. A message-passing
            model passes information one bond-length further per layer, so a naive split would need copies from five bond-lengths
            away. SevenNet instead has the GPUs swap their freshly computed atom descriptions after every layer, so a thin border is
            always enough, and then swaps the corresponding gradients on the way back to get the forces right.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="depth depth-tech">
          <p>
            Forward communication supplies <i>h</i><sup>(<i>t</i>)</sup> of ghosts so Eq. (2) can be evaluated for owned atoms on every
            layer. Reverse communication is reverse-mode differentiation across processors. A ghost’s ∂<i>E</i>/∂<i>h</i> computed
            locally is a partial sum (Eq. 4) that must be added to its owner’s before the next layer down, and the edge forces of
            Eq. (3) computed on ghosts are partial forces in the LAMMPS sense. Energies are pooled globally. The scheme leaves the model
            unchanged, so serial and parallel runs agree to rounding <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 2.2" />. The stress is
            available in serial mode <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Sec. 3.2" />.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="depth depth-res">
          <p>
            The scheme converts the receptive-field problem of message passing into a latency and bandwidth problem: 2(<i>T</i> − 1)
            extra synchronising exchanges per force call, each the size of the ghost shell times the feature width. That is why
            efficiency rises with model size in weak scaling and why strong scaling saturates once a GPU holds too few atoms to stay
            busy <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Secs. 4.1–4.2" />. Strictly local designs such as <a href="/models/allegro/">Allegro</a>
            avoid the exchanges altogether, at the cost of losing message passing. Multi-fidelity training is complementary: by
            sharing all weights but a few fidelity biases and shifts, the scarce high-fidelity data only have to teach the
            difference from a common surface, which the authors find more data-efficient than transfer learning or Δ-learning
            <Cite id="kim2024multifidelity" locator="Sec. 2.1; Fig. 1e; Sec. 2.2" />.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="out-h">
      <h2 id="out-h" class="h-section">Outputs</h2>
      <p>
        Energy, with forces as its gradient and stress from the virial <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Secs. 2, 3.2, 5.1" />. Models
        with several fidelities return the quantities of the fidelity selected at inference, so energies from different tasks are on
        different reference scales <Cite id="kim2024multifidelity" locator="Sec. 2.1" />. The package also provides a CUDA D3 dispersion
        correction that can be added to the energy (README). There are no charges and no electrostatic term.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="fail-h">
      <h2 id="fail-h" class="h-section">Where it can fail</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>From the architecture.</strong> The receptive field ends at <i>T</i>·<i>r</i><sub>c</sub>: 25 Å for SevenNet-0, but
        Coulomb tails, image charges and LO–TO splitting lie beyond any finite cutoff (<a href="/physics-lab/ewald/">P6</a>;
        <a href="/failures/electrostatics-at-electrodes/">Failure Atlas</a>). The parallel algorithm adds no error, but it adds
        exchanges. With few atoms per GPU it scales poorly, and the authors expect synchronisation costs to grow in heterogeneous
        systems, which their homogeneous quartz benchmarks did not test <Cite id="park2024sevennet" locator="Secs. 4.1–4.2" />. A
        fidelity that enters only as an energy shift cannot correct forces (Fig. 3).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>From a checkpoint.</strong> The released SevenNet-0 (11Jul2024) is not the model of the paper. It has the same
        architecture but was retrained on MPtrj, a near-equilibrium PBE/PBE+U dataset with the softening that implies (<a href="/failures/pes-softening/">Failure Atlas</a>).
        SevenNet-MF-0 shows strongly softened optical phonons because the Materials Project r²SCAN relaxations it learned from start at
        PBE minima and contain little force and stress information <Cite id="kim2024multifidelity" locator="Sec. 2.3" />. In
        multi-task checkpoints each task inherits its database’s DFT settings, so tasks are not interchangeable
        (<a href="/failures/experiment-vs-dft/">reference inheritance</a>).
      </p>
    </section>

