                  <td><a href={m.checkpoint_url}>{m.version}</a></td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small">
        The first two rows are Atlas records; the parameter counts come from the Matbench Discovery model cards. The other rows are the
        SevenNet models submitted to Matbench Discovery (snapshot {snapshot.source.commit_date}; leaderboard parameter counts,
        training-set labels and licences), whose results are on the <a href="/benchmarks/">Benchmarks</a> page. The repository is MIT
        licensed today, while the leaderboard lists GPL-3.0 for the older submissions. The documentation also lists SevenNet-l3i5
        (<i>l</i><sub>max</sub> = 3), SevenNet-omat, SevenNet-Omni with 13 tasks from 15 databases and its deeper i8 and i12
        variants, and the distilled SevenNet-Nano; these are not yet recorded here individually. SevenNet-0 is one of the four
        checkpoints in the <a href="/observatory/">Observatory</a>.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="note" title="Related families">
        <a href="/models/nequip/">NequIP</a> is the architecture SevenNet runs. <a href="/models/allegro/">Allegro</a> answers the same
        scaling problem the other way, by giving up message passing. <a href="/models/mace/">MACE</a> is another two-body-message
        network to which the authors say the scheme applies.
      </Callout>
    </section>

